WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.409
Here is the reader and the ledger writer from lesson 1.

2
00:00:06.409 --> 00:00:10.927
As a plain flow, it prints read, posted, read, posted, all on main.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:16.181
Now add one line: flowOn(io), a dispatcher with one thread, io-thread.

4
00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:18.837
Each ledger write takes 30 milliseconds.

5
00:00:18.837 --> 00:00:21.559
In what order do the six lines print now?

6
00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:28.026
flowOn(io) changes the dispatcher, so it builds a wall.

7
00:00:28.026 --> 00:00:34.203
Everything above it runs behind the wall, in a second coroutine on io-thread: a second cook.

8
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.923
In the wall sits a mailbox with 64 slots, a channel.

9
00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:42.084
The second cook's emit now just drops a plate in, and returns.

10
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.224
In front of the wall, a loop on main takes the plates out, one at a time, and calls your collector.

11
00:00:48.224 --> 00:00:49.780
The collector never moves.

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.836
So the reader runs ahead: all three reads print before the first posting is done.

13
00:00:54.836 --> 00:00:56.925
Then the postings come out on main.

14
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:00.109
The mailbox can fill up.

15
00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:06.413
With 64 plates in it and one in the diner's hands, the next send suspends until a slot frees up.

16
00:01:06.413 --> 00:01:08.252
Backpressure didn't go away.

17
00:01:08.252 --> 00:01:12.192
The reader now waits for a free slot, not for the collector.

18
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.765
Why build a wall at all?

19
00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:18.511
Why not wrap the read and the emit in withContext(io)?

20
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:21.362
Remember, the collector runs inside emit.

21
00:01:21.362 --> 00:01:25.402
An emit on io-thread would drag your collector behind the wall.

22
00:01:25.402 --> 00:01:29.121
Your ledger code would run on a thread it never asked for.

23
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:34.255
So the inspector from lesson 2 sounds the alarm: Flow invariant is violated.

24
00:01:34.255 --> 00:01:41.443
To move the reader and not the collector, you must break the call chain, with a second coroutine and a mailbox.

25
00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:49.541
Here is the Kotlin, and its Java twin: a reader thread fills a 64 slot queue, and main empties it.

26
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:56.188
So flowOn moves the code above it behind a wall, with a 64 slot channel in between.

27
00:01:56.188 --> 00:01:58.572
Your collector stays where it was.

28
00:01:58.800 --> 00:01:59.685
Quick check.

29
00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:06.542
Behind flowOn(io), a reader emits 100 lines at once, and the collector is stuck on the first.

30
00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:08.164
How many emits return?
